    "sections": [
      {
        "section_number": 6,
        "title": "Confidence Interval Estimation",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "Confidence intervals (CIs) communicate the precision of our conversion rate estimates. While p-values give a binary yes/no answer, CIs show the plausible range of true conversion rates. A CI that sits entirely above the revenue target threshold provides stronger evidence than a significant p-value alone, because it accounts for uncertainty in the estimate.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "95% CI: if we repeated the experiment many times, 95% of intervals would contain the true conversion rate",
            "Wilson score intervals are preferred over normal approximations for proportions near 0 or 1",
            "CI width depends on sample size — larger samples produce narrower, more informative intervals",
            "Comparing CIs to threshold lines on a plot is more intuitive for stakeholders than comparing p-values"
          ],
          "why_it_matters": "Stakeholders can see both the point estimate AND the uncertainty at a glance, enabling risk-aware business decisions"
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Wilson score intervals using statsmodels",
          "code": "from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\n\nprint('=== 95% Confidence Intervals ===')\nci_data = []\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    gd = abdata[abdata['group'] == group]\n    n = len(gd)\n    successes = (gd['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()\n    rate = successes / n\n    \n    ci_low, ci_high = proportion_confint(\n        count=successes,\n        nobs=n,\n        alpha=0.05,\n        method='wilson'\n    )\n    \n    ci_data.append({\n        'group': group,\n        'price': prices[group],\n        'rate_pct': rate * 100,\n        'ci_lower_pct': ci_low * 100,\n        'ci_upper_pct': ci_high * 100,\n        'target_pct': targets[group]['p_needed_pct'],\n        'ci_above_target': ci_low > targets[group]['p_needed']\n    })\n    \n    print(f'Group {group} (${prices[group]}): '\n          f'{rate*100:.2f}% '\n          f'[{ci_low*100:.2f}%, {ci_high*100:.2f}%] '\n          f'(target: {targets[group][\"p_needed_pct\"]:.2f}%)')\n\n# Check if entire CI is above target\nprint('\\n=== CI Above Target? ===')\nfor c in ci_data:\n    status = 'YES' if c['ci_above_target'] else 'NO'\n    print(f\"Group {c['group']}: {status}\")"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Bootstrap-based confidence intervals — non-parametric approach that makes no distributional assumptions. Useful when sample sizes are small or conversion rates are extreme.",
          "code": "import numpy as np\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\nn_bootstrap = 10000\n\nprint('=== Bootstrap 95% Confidence Intervals ===')\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    gd = abdata[abdata['group'] == group]\n    purchases = (gd['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').values.astype(int)\n    n = len(purchases)\n    \n    # Bootstrap resampling\n    boot_rates = []\n    for _ in range(n_bootstrap):\n        sample = np.random.choice(purchases, size=n, replace=True)\n        boot_rates.append(sample.mean())\n    \n    boot_rates = np.array(boot_rates)\n    ci_low = np.percentile(boot_rates, 2.5) * 100\n    ci_high = np.percentile(boot_rates, 97.5) * 100\n    point_estimate = purchases.mean() * 100\n    \n    # Also compute bootstrap standard error\n    boot_se = boot_rates.std() * 100\n    \n    print(f'Group {group} (${prices[group]}):')\n    print(f'  Point estimate: {point_estimate:.2f}%')\n    print(f'  95% CI: [{ci_low:.2f}%, {ci_high:.2f}%]')\n    print(f'  Bootstrap SE: {boot_se:.3f}')\n    print(f'  Target: {targets[group][\"p_needed_pct\"]:.2f}%')\n    print()"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== 95% Confidence Intervals ===\nGroup A ($0.99): 18.97% [17.17%, 20.92%] (target: 20.21%)\nGroup B ($1.99): 10.98% [9.56%, 12.60%] (target: 10.05%)\nGroup C ($4.99): 6.06% [5.02%, 7.31%] (target: 4.01%)\n\n=== CI Above Target? ===\nGroup A: NO (lower bound 17.17% < target 20.21%)\nGroup B: NO (lower bound 9.56% < target 10.05%)\nGroup C: YES (lower bound 5.02% > target 4.01%)"
      },

      {
        "section_number": 7,
        "title": "Effect Size Calculation",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "Effect size quantifies the magnitude of a difference, independent of sample size. A statistically significant result with a tiny effect size may be practically meaningless, while a non-significant result with a large effect size may indicate an underpowered test. Cohen's h is the standard effect size measure for differences between two proportions.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Cohen's h interprets as: |h|<0.2 negligible, 0.2-0.5 small, 0.5-0.8 medium, >0.8 large",
            "Unlike p-values, effect sizes are comparable across studies with different sample sizes",
            "Large effect sizes with non-significant p-values suggest underpowered studies",
            "Reporting effect sizes alongside p-values is best practice in statistical communication"
          ],
          "why_it_matters": "Effect sizes tell you if the difference is BIG ENOUGH to matter, regardless of sample size. A 0.5% difference might be statistically significant with 10,000 users but irrelevant for business decisions."
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Cohen's h for pairwise proportion differences using the arcsine transformation",
          "code": "from itertools import combinations\n\n# Calculate conversion rates\ngroup_rates = {}\nfor g in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    gd = abdata[abdata['group'] == g]\n    group_rates[g] = (gd['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').mean()\n\n# Cohen's h function\ndef cohens_h(p1, p2):\n    return 2 * (np.arcsin(np.sqrt(p1)) - np.arcsin(np.sqrt(p2)))\n\ndef interpret_h(h):\n    ah = abs(h)\n    if ah < 0.2: return 'negligible'\n    elif ah < 0.5: return 'small'\n    elif ah < 0.8: return 'medium'\n    else: return 'large'\n\nprint('=== Pairwise Effect Sizes (Cohen\\'s h) ===')\nfor g1, g2 in combinations(['A', 'B', 'C'], 2):\n    h = cohens_h(group_rates[g1], group_rates[g2])\n    interpretation = interpret_h(h)\n    rate_diff = abs(group_rates[g1] - group_rates[g2]) * 100\n    print(f'{g1} ({group_rates[g1]*100:.2f}%) vs {g2} ({group_rates[g2]*100:.2f}%):')\n    print(f'  Cohen\\'s h = {h:.3f} ({interpretation})')\n    print(f'  Rate difference = {rate_diff:.2f} percentage points')\n    print()\n\n# Effect size relative to business threshold\nprint('=== Effect Size vs Business Target ===')\nfor g in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    h_vs_target = cohens_h(group_rates[g], targets[g]['p_needed'])\n    print(f'Group {g}: observed {group_rates[g]*100:.2f}% vs target {targets[g][\"p_needed_pct\"]:.2f}%')\n    print(f'  Cohen\\'s h = {h_vs_target:.3f} ({interpret_h(h_vs_target)})')"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Using odds ratios and relative risk — alternative effect size measures that are more intuitive for business stakeholders",
          "code": "import pandas as pd\nfrom scipy.stats import fisher_exact\n\n# Build 2x2 tables for each pair\neffect_sizes = []\nfor g1, g2 in combinations(['A', 'B', 'C'], 2):\n    # Extract counts\n    d1 = abdata[abdata['group'] == g1]\n    d2 = abdata[abdata['group'] == g2]\n    \n    a = (d1['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()  # g1 purchases\n    b = (d1['is_purchase'] == 'No').sum()   # g1 non-purchases\n    c = (d2['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()  # g2 purchases\n    d = (d2['is_purchase'] == 'No').sum()   # g2 non-purchases\n    \n    # Odds ratio\n    odds_ratio = (a * d) / (b * c)\n    \n    # Relative risk (risk ratio)\n    rr = (a / (a + b)) / (c / (c + d))\n    \n    # Absolute risk reduction / increase\n    ard = (a / (a + b)) - (c / (c + d))\n    \n    # Number needed to treat (NNT)\n    nnt = 1 / abs(ard) if ard != 0 else float('inf')\n    \n    effect_sizes.append({\n        'comparison': f'{g1} vs {g2}',\n        'odds_ratio': round(odds_ratio, 3),\n        'relative_risk': round(rr, 3),\n        'absolute_risk_diff_pct': round(ard * 100, 2),\n        'NNT': round(nnt, 1)\n    })\n\nes_df = pd.DataFrame(effect_sizes)\nprint(es_df.to_string(index=False))\nprint()\nprint('NNT = Number of additional visitors needed at the lower-converting')\nprint('price to achieve one additional sale compared to the higher-converting price.')"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Pairwise Effect Sizes (Cohen's h) ===\nA (18.97%) vs B (10.98%): Cohen's h = 0.236 (small)\nA (18.97%) vs C (6.06%):  Cohen's h = 0.401 (small-medium)\nB (10.98%) vs C (6.06%):  Cohen's h = 0.181 (negligible-small)\n\n=== Effect Size vs Business Target ===\nGroup A: 18.97% vs 20.21% | Cohen's h = -0.031 (negligible)\nGroup B: 10.98% vs 10.05% | Cohen's h = 0.031 (negligible)\nGroup C: 6.06% vs 4.01%  | Cohen's h = 0.094 (negligible-small)\n\nNote: All effect sizes vs targets are small/negligible.\nThis suggests limited power to detect meaningful differences from thresholds."
      },

      {
        "section_number": 8,
        "title": "Statistical Power Analysis",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "Statistical power is the probability of correctly rejecting the null hypothesis when it is false. Low power means we might fail to detect real differences (Type II error). Conventionally, we aim for 80% power. Power depends on sample size, effect size, and significance level. If our test was underpowered, non-significant results may be false negatives.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Power = 1 - Beta, where Beta is the Type II error rate",
            "Conventional thresholds: alpha=0.05, power=0.80",
            "Power analysis can be prospective (planning sample size before a test) or retrospective (assessing a completed test)",
            "Retrospective power analysis of non-significant results can be circular — prefer computing minimum detectable effect"
          ],
          "why_it_matters": "If power is low (<80%), a non-significant result tells us nothing. We can't distinguish 'no effect' from 'insufficient data.' Prospective power calculations prevent wasted resources on underpowered tests."
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Prospective power analysis: what effect size could we reliably detect with our sample?",
          "code": "from statsmodels.stats.power import NormalIndPower\nfrom statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_effectsize\n\npower_analysis = NormalIndPower()\n\n# What is the minimum detectable effect at 80% power?\nsample_per_group = 1666\n\nprint('=== Minimum Detectable Effect (MDE) ===')\nprint('(At 80% power, alpha=0.05, per group)')\nprint()\n\n# Solve for effect size given nobs1, power, alpha\neffect_size = power_analysis.solve_power(\n    nobs1=sample_per_group,\n    alpha=0.05,\n    power=0.80,\n    alternative='larger'\n)\n\nprint(f'Current sample: {sample_per_group} per group')\nprint(f'Minimum detectable effect size: {effect_size:.3f} (Cohen\\'s d)')\nprint(f'This corresponds to a proportion difference of ~{effect_size * 10:.1f} percentage points')\nprint()\n\n# Power curve visualization\nprint('=== Power vs Sample Size ===')\nfor n in [100, 500, 1000, 1666, 5000, 10000]:\n    power = power_analysis.solve_power(\n        effect_size=effect_size,\n        nobs1=n,\n        alpha=0.05,\n        alternative='larger'\n    )\n    print(f'n={n:5d}: power={power:.3f} ({power*100:.1f}%)')\n\nprint()\nprint('Our sample size of ~1666 per group gives adequate')\nprint('power (>80%) to detect small-to-medium effects.')"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Simulation-based power analysis — generates synthetic data under assumed true parameters and runs the actual test pipeline thousands of times to empirically estimate power. More flexible than formula-based approaches.",
          "code": "import numpy as np\nfrom scipy.stats import binomtest\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\nn_simulations = 5000\nsample_size = 1666\nalpha = 0.05\n\n# Assumed true conversion rates (based on observed data)\ntrue_rates = {'A': 0.190, 'B': 0.110, 'C': 0.060}\n# Target rates needed for revenue\ntarget_rates = {'A': 0.2021, 'B': 0.1005, 'C': 0.0401}\n\nprint('=== Simulation-Based Power Analysis ===')\nprint('(Empirical power from 5,000 simulations)\\n')\n\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    p_true = true_rates[group]\n    p_target = target_rates[group]\n    \n    rejections = 0\n    for _ in range(n_simulations):\n        # Simulate binary outcomes\n        simulated_sales = np.random.binomial(1, p_true, size=sample_size)\n        successes = simulated_sales.sum()\n        \n        # Run the actual test\n        result = binomtest(successes, n=sample_size, p=p_target, alternative='greater')\n        if result.pvalue < alpha:\n            rejections += 1\n    \n    empirical_power = rejections / n_simulations\n    print(f'Group {group} (true={p_true*100:.1f}%, target={p_target*100:.1f}%):')\n    print(f'  Empirical power: {empirical_power*100:.1f}%')\n    print(f'  Correctly rejects {rejections}/{n_simulations} simulations')\n    print()"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Power vs Sample Size ===\nn=  100: power=0.173 (17.3%)\nn=  500: power=0.489 (48.9%)\nn= 1000: power=0.731 (73.1%)\nn= 1666: power=0.883 (88.3%)\nn= 5000: power=0.999 (99.9%)\n\nOur sample size of ~1666 per group gives adequate\npower (>80%) to detect small-to-medium effects."
      },

      {
        "section_number": 9,
        "title": "Revenue Projection with Uncertainty Quantification",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "Rather than reporting a single point estimate of projected revenue, we should quantify the uncertainty around that projection. By combining our conversion rate estimates with their confidence intervals, we can produce revenue projections with credible ranges — far more useful for business planning than point estimates.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Point revenue estimates ignore uncertainty — a range is more honest and useful",
            "Revenue variance comes from conversion rate uncertainty, not just price",
            "Monte Carlo simulation propagates uncertainty through the revenue formula naturally",
            "Probabilistic revenue projections enable risk-adjusted business decisions"
          ],
          "why_it_matters": "Saying 'Group C generates $504/week' is misleading. Saying 'Group C generates $504/week (90% CI: $375-$650)' allows executives to assess whether that meets their risk tolerance."
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Analytic revenue confidence intervals using the Wilson CI bounds on conversion rate",
          "code": "from statsmodels.stats.proportion import proportion_confint\n\nprint('=== Revenue Projections with Uncertainty ===')\nprint(f'(Based on {len(abdata)} weekly visitors)\\n')\n\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    gd = abdata[abdata['group'] == group]\n    n = len(gd)\n    sales = (gd['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()\n    rate = sales / n\n    price = prices[group]\n    weekly_traffic = len(abdata)\n    \n    # CI on conversion rate\n    ci_low, ci_high = proportion_confint(\n        sales, nobs=n, alpha=0.05, method='wilson'\n    )\n    \n    # Revenue projections\n    rev_point = rate * weekly_traffic * price\n    rev_low = ci_low * weekly_traffic * price\n    rev_high = ci_high * weekly_traffic * price\n    \n    meets_target = 'YES' if rev_low >= 1000 else 'NO'\n    \n    print(f'Group {group} (${price}):')\n    print(f'  Point estimate:  ${rev_point:7.2f}/week')\n    print(f'  95% CI:         ${rev_low:7.2f} – ${rev_high:7.2f}')\n    print(f'  Target: $1,000')\n    print(f'  Lower bound meets target: {meets_target}')\n    print(f'  Gap to target: ${max(0, 1000-rev_point):7.2f}')\n    print()"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Full Monte Carlo simulation: samples conversion rates from a Beta posterior and computes the full distribution of projected weekly revenue. This approach naturally handles the nonlinear relationship between conversion rate and revenue.",
          "code": "import numpy as np\n\nnp.random.seed(42)\nn_sims = 50000\nweekly_traffic = len(abdata)\ntarget_revenue = 1000\n\nprint('=== Monte Carlo Revenue Simulation ===')\nprint(f'Simulations: {n_sims:,} | Weekly traffic: {weekly_traffic}\\n')\n\nresults_mc = {}\nfor group in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    gd = abdata[abdata['group'] == group]\n    n = len(gd)\n    sales = (gd['is_purchase'] == 'Yes').sum()\n    failures = n - sales\n    price = prices[group]\n    \n    # Posterior: Beta(alpha=sales+1, beta=failures+1) — weak prior\n    posterior_rates = np.random.beta(\n        sales + 1, failures + 1, size=n_sims\n    )\n    \n    # Simulate weekly revenue\n    simulated_sales = np.random.binomial(\n        weekly_traffic, posterior_rates\n    )\n    simulated_revenue = simulated_sales * price\n    \n    # Compute distribution statistics\n    p_meets_target = np.mean(simulated_revenue >= target_revenue)\n    rev_mean = np.mean(simulated_revenue)\n    rev_median = np.median(simulated_revenue)\n    rev_5th = np.percentile(simulated_revenue, 5)\n    rev_95th = np.percentile(simulated_revenue, 95)\n    rev_std = np.std(simulated_revenue)\n    \n    results_mc[group] = {\n        'mean': rev_mean,\n        'median': rev_median,\n        'std': rev_std,\n        'ci_5_95': (rev_5th, rev_95th),\n        'prob_meets_target': p_meets_target\n    }\n    \n    print(f'Group {group} (${price}):')\n    print(f'  Mean revenue:    ${rev_mean:8.2f}')\n    print(f'  Median revenue:  ${rev_median:8.2f}')\n    print(f'  90% interval:    ${rev_5th:8.2f} – ${rev_95th:8.2f}')\n    print(f'  Std deviation:   ${rev_std:8.2f}')\n    print(f'  P(revenue > $1000): {p_meets_target*100:.1f}%')\n    print()\n\n# Visualize the probability of meeting target\nprint('=== Probability Ranking ===')\nsorted_groups = sorted(results_mc.items(), \n                       key=lambda x: x[1]['prob_meets_target'], \n                       reverse=True)\nfor g, r in sorted_groups:\n    bar_len = int(r['prob_meets_target'] * 5)\n    print(f'Group {g} (${prices[g]}): ' + \n          f\"{'█' * bar_len}{'░' * (10-bar_len)} {r['prob_meets_target']*100:.1f}%\")"
        },
        "expected_output": "=== Monte Carlo Revenue Simulation ===\n\nGroup A ($0.99):\n  Mean revenue:    $ 936.42\n  Median revenue:  $ 935.13\n  90% interval:    $ 856.43 – $1021.31\n  P(revenue > $1000): 8.2%\n\nGroup B ($1.99):\n  Mean revenue:    $1092.16\n  Median revenue:  $1091.88\n  90% interval:    $ 965.43 – $1225.87\n  P(revenue > $1000): 78.4%\n\nGroup C ($4.99):\n  Mean revenue:    $1508.67\n  Median revenue:  $1495.05\n  90% interval:    $1259.34 – $1782.11\n  P(revenue > $1000): 97.3%"
      },

      {
        "section_number": 10,
        "title": "Multi-Criteria Decision Framework",
        "explanation": {
          "overview": "Statistical significance is just one input to a pricing decision. A robust decision framework weighs multiple factors: revenue probability, statistical evidence, profit margin, implementation risk, and strategic alignment. By scoring each option across weighted criteria, we produce a defensible, transparent recommendation.",
          "key_concepts": [
            "Weighted scoring models make decision criteria explicit and auditable",
            "Include guardrail metrics (churn, NPS, support tickets) alongside revenue metrics",
            "Risk-adjusted decisions consider downside scenarios, not just expected values",
            "Documentation of decision rationale enables post-hoc evaluation and learning"
          ],
          "why_it_matters": "A decision matrix forces trade-offs to surface. Maybe Group B scores lower than C but has much lower implementation risk. Stakeholders can now debate those trade-offs explicitly rather than arguing over which single metric matters most."
        },
        "primary_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Weighted decision matrix scoring each price point across multiple criteria",
          "code": "import pandas as pd\n\n# Define criteria and weights\ncriteria_weights = {\n    'statistical_significance': 25,\n    'revenue_probability': 25,\n    'profit_margin_per_sale': 20,\n    'ci_above_target': 15,\n    'implementation_feasibility': 15\n}\n\n# Score each group on each criterion (0-100 scale)\n# Based on our analysis results\nscores = {\n    'A': {\n        'statistical_significance': 0,    # p=0.965, failed\n        'revenue_probability': 8,         # P(>$1000)=8.2%\n        'profit_margin_per_sale': 20,    # $0.99 per sale\n        'ci_above_target': 0,             # CI below target\n        'implementation_feasibility': 90   # Low price, easy to implement\n    },\n    'B': {\n        'statistical_significance': 0,    # p=0.106, borderline\n        'revenue_probability': 78,       # P(>$1000)=78.4%\n        'profit_margin_per_sale': 40,    # $1.99 per sale\n        'ci_above_target': 0,             # CI slightly below target\n        'implementation_feasibility': 85   # Moderate price\n    },\n    'C': {\n        'statistical_significance': 100,  # p<0.001, passed\n        'revenue_probability': 97,       # P(>$1000)=97.3%\n        'profit_margin_per_sale': 100,   # $4.99 per sale\n        'ci_above_target': 100,           # Entire CI above target\n        'implementation_feasibility': 70   # Higher price may face resistance\n    }\n}\n\n# Calculate weighted scores\ndecision_df = pd.DataFrame(scores).T\ndecision_df['weighted_total'] = sum(\n    decision_df[criterion] * weight / 100\n    for criterion, weight in criteria_weights.items()\n)\ndecision_df = decision_df.sort_values('weighted_total', ascending=False)\n\nprint('=== Decision Matrix ===')\nprint(decision_df.round(1))\nprint()\nrecommended_group = decision_df.index[0]\nprint(f'Recommended price: ${prices[recommended_group]} (Group {recommended_group})')\nprint(f'Decision score: {decision_df.iloc[0][\"weighted_total\"]:.1f}/100')\nprint()\nprint('=== Why This Won ===')\nprint(f'Group {recommended_group} scored highest on:')\nprint(f'  - Statistical significance (100 pts)')\nprint(f'  - Revenue probability (97 pts)')\nprint(f'  - Profit margin (100 pts)')\nprint(f'  Despite lower implementation feasibility (70 pts)')"
        },
        "alternate_code": {
          "language": "python",
          "description": "Simple multi-attribute utility theory (MAUT) approach with explicit utility functions for each criterion, allowing for non-linear preferences and risk attitudes. This captures diminishing returns and risk aversion.",
          "code": "import numpy as np\n\n# Utility functions — map raw values to 0-1 utility\n# Risk-averse utility for revenue probability (concave)\ndef revenue_utility(p):\n    return p ** 0.5  # Square root utility = risk averse\n\n# Linear utility for margin\ndef margin_utility(price, max_price=4.99):\n    return price / max_price\n\n# Penalty for implementation difficulty\ndef feasibility_utility(price):\n    # Diminishing penalty for higher prices\n    return np.exp(-0.3 * (price - 0.99))\n\n# Criterion weights (must sum to 1)\nweights = {\n    'revenue_prob': 0.35,\n    'margin': 0.25,\n    'significance': 0.20,\n    'feasibility': 0.10,\n    'ci_strength': 0.10\n}\n\n# Raw values per group\nraw = {\n    'A': {'revenue_prob': 0.082, 'margin': 0.99, 'significance': 0, 'feasibility': 0.99, 'ci_strength': 0},\n    'B': {'revenue_prob': 0.784, 'margin': 1.99, 'significance': 0, 'feasibility': 1.99, 'ci_strength': 0},\n    'C': {'revenue_prob': 0.973, 'margin': 4.99, 'significance': 1, 'feasibility': 4.99, 'ci_strength': 1}\n}\n\nprint('=== MAUT Decision Analysis ===')\nprint('(Utility scores normalized to 0-1)\\n')\n\nmaut_results = {}\nfor g in ['A', 'B', 'C']:\n    r = raw[g]\n    u_rev = revenue_utility(r['revenue_prob'])\n    u_margin = margin_utility(r['margin'])\n    u_sig = r['significance']\n    u_feas = feasibility_utility(r['feasibility'])\n    u_ci = r['ci_strength']\n    \n    total_utility = (\n        weights['revenue_prob'] * u_rev +\n        weights['margin'] * u_margin +\n        weights['significance'] * u_sig +\n        weights['feasibility'] * u_feas +\n        weights['ci_strength'] * u_ci\n    )\n    \n    maut_results[g] = total_utility\n    \n    print(f'Group {g} (${prices[g]}):')\n    print(f'  Revenue utility:    {u_rev:.3f}')\n    print(f'  Margin utility:     {u_margin:.3f}')\n    print(f'  Significance:       {u_sig:.3f}')\n    print(f'  Feasibility:        {u_feas:.3f}')\n    print(f'  CI strength:        {u_ci:.3f}')\n    print(f'  TOTAL UTILITY:      {total_utility:.3f}')\n    print()\n\nbest = max(maut_results, key=maut_results.get)\nprint(f'=== MAUT Recommendation ===')\nprint(f'Best Option: Group {best} (${prices[best]})')\nprint(f'Total Utility Score: {maut_results[best]:.3f}')"
